title: What the slow log records
tags: [[Slow log]]
kind: reference
summary: One JSON line per slow operation — when, how long, how many keys, its context and its phases — and what is left out.

One JSON object per line, written when the operation ends:

```
{"at_ms": 1757426602000, "source": "daemon", "op": "POST /repos/:repo/query",
 "ms": 4820, "reads": 210314, "op_id": "9f3c1d0a4b7e4f2a",
 "phases": [{"name": "wait:conn", "ms": 3100, "count": 1, "depth": 0},
            {"name": "prepare", "ms": 1650, "count": 1, "depth": 0, "reads": 210008},
            {"name": "index.evaluate", "ms": 40, "count": 1, "depth": 0, "reads": 306}],
 "context": {"client": "mfr_path ->* \"/2024\" and rating > 3",
             "query": "{\"type\":\"and\",…}", "results": "1240"}}
```

|!Field |!Meaning |
|`at_ms` |when the operation ''started'' (Unix ms): sorting by it puts an operation next to the ones it waited for |
|`source` |`daemon` or `gui`: which process timed it |
|`op` |for an HTTP request, the method and the ''route pattern'' (`POST /repos/:repo/query`), never the concrete URL: a shape to group by |
|`ms` |the total, end to end, as the recording process measured it |
|`reads` |the keys the store read during the operation (absent when none, as on the GUI's entries) |
|`op_id` |the id correlating a GUI entry with the daemon's for the same request, when the client sent one |
|`phases` |the breakdown, below |
|`context` |free-form pairs, in order: the query, the number of results, the field written, the events of a flush. A query is kept whole (up to 64 KiB) with its `sort`, `limit` and `count`, so the entry can be replayed |

!! Phases

A phase is a named span declared by the code that runs it. Durations are
''inclusive'' — a phase containing another counts its time too — and `depth` says
how deeply it is nested. Occurrences of one name are summed, `count` saying how
many: a write validating the schema once per metarecord reports one
`validate.schema` of 2.4 s with `count: 900`. Phases keep the order they first
appeared in, so they retell the operation. Time outside every top-level phase is
not padded with an "other": a gap that matters is a missing instrumentation site,
and naming it would hide that. Each phase also carries its `reads`, inclusive
like its time.

The daemon's vocabulary is stable — a new site adds to it rather than inventing a
synonym:

|!Phase |!Covers |
|`wait:conn` |waiting for the repository's store handle |
|`prepare` |resolving path targets, exact nodes and the forest's leaves of a query |
|`index.evaluate` |evaluating the page (and count) over the store |
|`assemble` |assembling the selected fields of a result page |
|`resolve.uuids` |resolving a write's query to its whole match set |
|`write.fields` |the field writes inside a transaction |
|`write.bulk_create` |creating a batch of metarecords |
|`write.trash_delete` |deleting the metarecords of a trashing |
|`validate.schema` |schema validation of a write |
|`commit` |committing the transaction (the store's `fsync`) |
|`rules.load` |re-reading the watch-rule index after HEAD moved |
|`settle.rules` |re-reading it after a write that moved a rule |
|`settle.watches` |recomputing the watched directories |
|`watcher.compact` |compacting a batch of buffered events |
|`watcher.apply` |applying one group of compacted events |

A `wait:*` phase is the one that distinguishes "this is expensive" from "this was
fine and something else held the repository".

!! What is left out

* Operations whose slowness is expected and already reported, as observable tasks: loading a repository, a full reconcile, the duplicate scan, the similarity phase.
* Operations that name no repository (`GET /health`, `POST /repos/load`): the log lives in a repository, and these have none.
